Good question. `dashboard-header` is just a name we chose. We could have called it anything — `top-bar`, `header-section`, `green-bar`. The name itself means nothing to HTML or CSS, it is just a label.

Where it is used in CSS — look at your `<style>` block, line 11:

```css
.dashboard-header {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--green-dark) 0%, var(--green-mid) 100%);
  color: #fff; padding: 18px 28px;
  display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.18);
}
```

The `.` before `dashboard-header` means "find any HTML element that has `class="dashboard-header"`" and apply these styles to it.

So the connection is:

```
HTML:  <div class="dashboard-header">   ← has the class
CSS:   .dashboard-header { ... }        ← styles anything with that class
```

The CSS `.` is how you link a style to an HTML element. That is how CSS works — you give an element a class name, then in CSS you write `.thatName { }` to style it.

We used the same name `dashboard-header` in `index.html` (the dashboard) so both pages share the same green header style — that is why they look consistent.

